# Service Conventions

## There Is No Service Layer

Rails does not have a service layer. Business logic belongs on the model. If you are reaching for `app/services/`, stop and ask where this logic *actually* lives.

```ruby
# Good — domain verb on the model; the Publication record is the single
# source of truth (its created_at is the publish time — no duplicate column)
class Article < ApplicationRecord
  def publish(by: Current.user)
    create_publication!(publisher: by)
  end
end

# Bad — service wrapping a model call
class PublishArticleService
  def call(article, user:)
    article.update!(published: true, published_by: user)
  end
end
```

This is about wrappers, not about the word. A **table-backed model** named
`Service`, `Operation` or `Command` is domain vocabulary — a row in a table, named
for what it stores — and it is the right place for behaviour, not a violation of
this rule. `AgentHarnessRails/ServiceObject` is scoped accordingly: it flags
`app/services/` and names that put a verb in front of a suffix
(`PublishArticleService`, `ApplicationService`), and leaves records alone.

## The Decision Tree

Before writing any service-like object, walk this tree:

```
Does this logic operate on a single model instance?
├── YES → Model method (domain verb)
│         e.g. article.publish, account.suspend
└── NO
    Does it create/validate records from form input?
    ├── YES → Form object (ActiveModel::Model)
    │         e.g. Registration, Checkout, Import
    └── NO
        Is it async work, scheduled, or retriable?
        ├── YES → Job (ActiveJob)
        │         e.g. ProcessPaymentJob, SyncInventoryJob
        └── NO
            Is there a primary model this coordinates around?
            ├── YES → PORO namespaced under that model
            │         e.g. Account::Onboarding, Order::Fulfillment
            └── NO
                Is this a noun your business uses in everyday language
                (not a technical operation dressed as a noun)?
                ├── YES → Standalone PORO in app/models/
                │         e.g. Search, Broadcast, Import
                │         Fails the test: ReportGenerator, PaymentProcessor,
                │         UserAuthenticator (verb phrases or infra wrappers)
                └── NO
                    Let the controller orchestrate inline.
```

**"Do I actually need a new object at all?"** One model, one action → model
method. Two models the controller can coordinate → inline orchestration is
fine. Extract only when you can name the concept precisely and the name is
richer than the model name alone. If you're struggling to name it, the logic
belongs on the model.

## Where Logic Actually Lives

| Situation | Where It Goes |
|-----------|---------------|
| Operates on a single model instance | Model method (domain verb) |
| Shared behaviour across models | Concern |
| Complex multi-step operation on one aggregate | PORO namespaced under the model |
| Multi-field form spanning models or workflow | Form object (`ActiveModel::Model`) |
| Async work, retries, scheduling | Job (`ActiveJob`) — **`agent_harness_rails/rules/jobs.mdc`**, **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-jobs/references/patterns.md`** |
| Incoming email processing | Mailbox (`ActionMailbox`) |
| Sending email | Mailer (`ActionMailer`) — **`agent_harness_rails/rules/mailers.mdc`**, **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-mailers/references/patterns.md`** |
| External API integration | PORO in `app/models/` namespaced under a domain noun |
| Controller coordinates two models | Inline controller orchestration — no wrapper needed |

A concern is a **mixin** — it gets included and becomes part of the model; use
one for behaviour intrinsic to what a model *is*, or shared across two or more
unrelated models. A PORO is an **operation** — it receives the model and
coordinates it; use one for a distinct workflow step within one aggregate.

## POROs Under Model Namespaces

When a model method grows too complex or an operation involves several steps within a single aggregate, extract to a PORO namespaced under the primary model. These live in `app/models/`, not `app/services/`.

- **Named as a noun** — the object *is* a concept, not an action
- **Initialised with its primary model** — the aggregate root, not a bag of dependencies
- **Methods are domain verbs** — `process`, `complete`, `import`, not `call` or `execute`
- **No base class** — each PORO stands alone; `ApplicationService` is an anti-pattern
- **Uses the model's transaction** — `account.transaction` keeps data integrity with the aggregate
- **Returns meaningful values** — the updated model or created records, not a result object
- **Lets exceptions propagate** — bang methods internally; the controller or caller handles the exception

A PORO is not a junk drawer: each public method must be a verb of the named concept; a method that operates on the model alone goes back on the model, presentation goes to a helper. A PORO that accumulates unrelated methods is a service object with a nicer name.

Worked examples and file organisation: **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-services/references/patterns.md`** § POROs.

## Form Objects for Multi-Model Writes

When a single form creates or updates records across multiple models, use a form object with `ActiveModel::Model`. This replaces both `accepts_nested_attributes_for` and service objects. Form objects live in `app/models/` — they are models, just not database-backed ones.

| Scenario | Use Form Object? |
|----------|-----------------|
| Single model, standard validations | No — use the model directly |
| Single model, workflow-specific validations | Yes — keeps model validations clean |
| Form spans two or more models | Yes — replaces `accepts_nested_attributes_for` |
| Wizard / multi-step form | Yes — one form object per step |
| Import from CSV / external data | Yes — validates before persisting |
| API payload mapping | Yes — transforms input to model attributes |

**Form object or PORO?** Receives user input and needs validation → form
object (`ActiveModel::Model`). Receives model instances and performs
operations → PORO. Both → form object that delegates to model methods after
validation.

Worked examples (create, update, controller and view integration): **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-services/references/patterns.md`** § Form Objects.

## Jobs Are Your Async Service Layer

Anything that should happen in the background is a job, not a service. The model enqueues (`after_create_commit`, always outside the open transaction), the job executes. Conventions: **`agent_harness_rails/rules/jobs.mdc`**.

## Naming and Placement

| Thing | Convention | Examples |
|-------|-----------|----------|
| POROs under models | Noun describing the operation | `Account::Onboarding`, `Order::Fulfillment` |
| Form objects | Noun describing the workflow | `Registration`, `Checkout`, `ContactForm` |
| Standalone domain objects | Domain noun | `Search`, `Broadcast`, `Import` |
| Methods | Domain verb | `process`, `complete`, `import`, `sync` |
| Jobs | `VerbNounJob` | `ProcessPaymentJob`, `SyncInventoryJob` |
| Exceptions | Namespaced under model | `Order::NotFulfillable`, `Account::AlreadySuspended` |

Name after a domain noun, not a verb: `Account::Onboarding`, not `OnboardAccount` or `OnboardService`.

Where files live:

- Form objects → `app/models/` (e.g. `app/models/registration.rb`)
- Model-namespaced POROs → `app/models/model_name/` (e.g. `app/models/order/fulfillment.rb`)
- Standalone domain objects → `app/models/` (e.g. `app/models/search.rb`)
- Jobs → `app/jobs/` (e.g. `app/jobs/process_payment_job.rb`)
- Never → `app/services/`

## Anti-Patterns

| Anti-Pattern | Instead |
|-------------|---------|
| `app/services/` directory | Model methods, POROs under models, form objects, jobs — all in `app/models/` or `app/jobs/` |
| Single-method `call` service objects | Model method or PORO with a named domain verb |
| Service wrapping one `update!` | Model domain verb |
| Service as a "transaction script" | Model method with `transaction` block |
| Service objects injecting models to simulate loose coupling | If you need the model, be the model or live under it — PORO constructor injection is fine; injection-as-architectural-boundary is not |
| `ApplicationService` base class | No base class needed — each PORO stands alone |
| `ServiceResult` / `Result` monads | Return the created/updated record, or raise on failure |
| Service for form validation | Form object (`ActiveModel::Model`) |
| Service for async work | Job (`ActiveJob`) |
| Service for email | Mailer (`ActionMailer`) — see mailers rule |
| Service per CRUD action | Controller + model handle CRUD natively |
| God service coordinating everything | Break into model methods; controller orchestrates |
| `Interactor`, `UseCase`, `Command` patterns | POROs with domain names — no framework needed |
| `begin/rescue` in service for flow control | Let exceptions propagate; `rescue_from` in controller |
| Generic `execute`, `run` method names on POROs and form objects | Domain verbs: `complete`, `process`, `import`. Exception: `perform` is the ActiveJob contract and must not be renamed. |

## Verification

Before finishing, verify:

- [ ] No `app/services/` directory — objects live in `app/models/` or `app/jobs/`
- [ ] No `ApplicationService` or service base class
- [ ] No single-method `call` — methods use domain verbs
- [ ] Objects are named as nouns, not verb phrases
- [ ] POROs are namespaced under their primary model
- [ ] Form objects include `ActiveModel::Model` and have validations
- [ ] Async work is in jobs, not POROs
- [ ] Objects that wrap a single model call have been inlined to model methods
- [ ] Transactions wrap the right scope — ideally on the model's own `transaction`
- [ ] The object represents a real domain concept, not a procedural wrapper
- [ ] Every PORO method earns its place — each public method is a verb of the named concept; single-model operations moved back to model methods, presentation to helpers
- [ ] No success/failure result monads — return records or raise exceptions (a small `Data.define` summary from a bulk operation is fine; a `Result` wrapper around every call is not)
